  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nAfter an extended duration of deep monsoon moisture over the\nBorderland, we are moving into a transition toward a drier regime\nas high pressure noses in from the east, shoving the deeper\nmoisture W and N, and bringing in drier air from the NE and E. As\nearly as tomorrow, we should see a downward trend in the coverage\nof showers and storms across the region, with a focus of further\nstorm activity over area high mountains and the N and W lowlands\nacross Southern New Mexico. PWAT has been near 1.5\", and it is\nproged to drop to near 1.0\", and dewpoints in the 60s are supposed\nto fall into the 40s. Both of those lower numbers are still\nrespectable enough to allow for a pop-up storm anywhere across \nthe CWFA, but the chances will drop for the southern and eastern \nareas of the forecast area. It will further drop Thursday and \nFriday as the upper high continues to expand west over the region,\nwith subsident warming aloft, adding a cap, suppressing \ninstability. Along with this \"drying trend\" will be an associated \nwarming trend, with lowland temperatures reaching toward the \ntriple digits as early as Thursday.\n\nLate weekend the upper high, if the GFS is correct, jogs to the\nnorth, which could allow a return of more moisture back into the\nregion. However, the high doesn't move as far north as it did a\ncouple weeks ago, to start this wet period, so it looks like the\nmoisture reintroduction will be somewhat limited, and still\nfocused over SW and W NE, with areas to the east somewhat dry\nstill. The ECMWF model still shows a upper high controlling the \nconditions over the region into next week, keeping the forecast \nwarmer and drier.",
